Assessment: the available `main` run history contains one run (run_301), which succeeded; its listed test, lint and build jobs also succeeded. No failed run was returned, so this history does not establish the cause of the reported transient failure. The token inventory still lists token_001, with last use at the same time the run started. That timing alone does not establish either legitimate use or compromise. The workflow references `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `CODECOV_TOKEN` and `SNYK_TOKEN`, but I could not establish that `GITHUB_TOKEN` is token_001 from the available information.

The available run details do not include job logs, and the token inventory does not provide an access audit trail, so exposure or unauthorized use cannot be ruled out. There is insufficient evidence to confirm compromise of token_001; I have not revoked it, since revocation would disrupt CI.

@marcus-chen @sarah-kim: Please review the relevant historical job logs and debug settings, token access/audit events, and the mapping between the workflow credentials and token_001. Also assess the other referenced secrets, and determine whether containment or rotation is warranted under the credential incident response procedure. Please avoid posting secret values or sensitive log output here.
